## File structure

telegram-bot-api/
  Client.cpp/.h           — main bot logic, all method handlers
  ClientManager.cpp/.h    — multi-bot management
  ClientParameters.h      — config/params
  HttpConnection.cpp/.h   — HTTP server
  HttpServer.h            — server setup
  WebhookActor.cpp/.h     — webhook delivery
  Query.cpp/.h            — request parsing
  Stats.cpp/.h            — statistics
  Watchdog.cpp/.h         — health monitoring
  telegram-bot-api.cpp    — main entry point

## Implications for TPR

1. Self-hosted = zero rate limit wall for streaming drafts
2. 2GB file uploads = code repos, datasets, model weights via Telegram
3. Local file paths = TPR reads/writes files directly, no HTTP upload
4. HTTP webhooks = TPR receives updates on localhost, no SSL needed
5. 100K webhook connections = massive scale for harness product
6. Full source = can study exact behavior of every method
7. C++ = can patch/extend if Telegram's implementation doesn't fit

## Integration with en0 stack

- Build on en0 (AMD HawkPoint has the CPU for C++ compile)
- Run as sysgenctl seat alongside cursor-proxy and TPR
- Webhook points to TPR on localhost (HTTP, no SSL)
- TPR calls localhost Bot API instead of api.telegram.org
- File operations use local paths (vec3/var/ for temp, _TRON for persist)

